      <h3>Which way should the wheel run?</h3>
      <p>Every jig in this kit rides on the universal support, and the first decision for any job is where the support goes. The T-8 has two sets of sleeves for it:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Vertical sleeves, edge leading.</strong> The support stands up in front of the stone and the wheel turns <em>into</em> the edge. This is the fastest grind, throws water away from you, and raises a smaller, weaker burr. It is the normal position for knives in the SVM-45, SVM-140 and SVM-00, and the only position for the SVX-150 scissors jig and SVA-170 axe jig. The risk is the edge digging into the stone, which is why every knife-jig instruction insists on keeping the jig stop pressed down against the support bar.</li>
        <li><strong>Horizontal sleeves, edge trailing.</strong> The support lies flat over the top of the stone and the wheel turns <em>away</em> from the edge, the same direction as the honing wheel. Grinding pressure is lower, the edge cannot catch, and you can see the burr forming because no water runs over it. This is how the SVS-38 is always used, and it is an option for short knives (mounted within about 50 mm / 2&quot; of the tip) when you only want a light touch-up. Long knives cannot be ground this way because the jig collides with the support leg.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svm00_p05_01.jpg" alt="Knife jig on the universal support in the vertical position, grinding toward the edge" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svm00_p05_03.jpg" alt="Knife jig on the universal support in the horizontal position, grinding away from the edge" />
      </div>
      <p>Bench height matters more than it sounds. Tormek suggests 550&ndash;650 mm (22&ndash;26&quot;) for grinding toward the edge and a taller bench (over 700 mm / 27-1/2&quot;) if you prefer to grind away from the edge; sitting on a stool in front of the machine is the recommended posture for scissors.</p>

      <h3>The general knife workflow</h3>
      <p>Every knife section below is a variation on the same eight steps. Learn them once with a cheap kitchen knife before putting anything you care about in the jig.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Decide what the knife needs.</strong> A knife that is merely dull keeps its angle and only needs a fine-graded stone and the leather wheel; a chipped or rolled edge, or one you want to re-angle, needs the coarse-graded stone first. Grade the stone with the SP-650 before you start (see the T-8 page).</li>
        <li><strong>Mount the knife in the jig</strong> so the edge is parallel to the jig and the clamp sits at the right spot along the blade (details per jig below). Tighten firmly; a slipping knife ruins the angle.</li>
        <li><strong>Set the edge angle</strong> by adjusting the universal support height with the Micro Adjust nut. Use the KS-123 for a chosen per-side angle, the WM-200 AngleMaster, or the Marker Method to copy the existing bevel.</li>
        <li><strong>Grind the first side</strong> with the jig stop pressed against the support, moving the blade slowly across the full width of the stone and raising the handle as you reach the curve of the tip. Keep going until a burr has formed along the <em>entire</em> edge, heel to tip. Feel for it by stroking a fingertip off the back of the blade across the edge (never along it); in good light it shows as a bright silver line.</li>
        <li><strong>Flip the jig over</strong> without unclamping the knife and grind the second side. The burr flips over almost immediately because it already exists; grind roughly as long as you did on the first side so the bevels stay symmetrical.</li>
        <li><strong>Refine.</strong> Re-grade the stone fine (if you started coarse) and take a few light passes on each side. A final very light pass on the first side thins the burr so it hones off easily.</li>
        <li><strong>Hone both sides on the leather wheel</strong>, edge trailing, at the same angle, with light pressure, alternating sides until the burr is gone. Then strop once more on each side.</li>
        <li><strong>Test.</strong> Slice printer paper from heel to tip; drag the edge through end grain to strip any remaining wire edge; check under a light for any shiny line of remaining flat on the apex.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="warning">
        Water-cooled grinding will not overheat a knife, but the stone will happily eat a fingertip. Keep fingers behind the jig and off the top of the wheel when grinding toward the edge, keep sleeves out of the way, and switch the machine off whenever you are setting angles with the KS-123 or AngleMaster.
      </div>

      <h3>The case and tray</h3>
      <p>The TC-800 is Tormek&rsquo;s stackable hard case; the HTK-00 is the foam insert cut for the hand-tool jigs. Dry the jigs before they go back in the tray: the stainless parts shrug off water but the foam will hold it against the steel knobs and screws for days. The spare slots are a sensible home for the KS-123 and a permanent marker.</p>
