        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/web_tc800.jpg" alt="Tormek TC-800 case closed, showing the clear lid and carry handle" />
        <figcaption>TC-800 case (Tormek product photo)</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h2>Kitchen and Chef&rsquo;s Knives &mdash; SVM-45</h2>
      <p>The SVM-45 is the workhorse. It is a flat clamp with a 45 mm wide jaw that grips the blade, plus a round shaft that rides on the universal support and ends in an adjustable stop. The stop is what you press down onto the support bar while you grind; the jig pivots around it, which is how you follow the curve of a chef&rsquo;s knife toward the tip without changing the angle. Tormek no longer publishes a separate leaflet for this jig; this section follows the <em>Knife Jig SVM-45</em> chapter of the HB-10 handbook (pages 52&ndash;58), and the figures are taken from it.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p52_01.jpg" alt="SVM-45 knife jig in use on a Tormek, with silhouettes of the kitchen knives and carver's drawknives it handles" />
        <figcaption>SVM-45 in use. Knives from 60 mm (2-3/8&quot;) blade length, and carver&rsquo;s drawknives</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p52_02.jpg" alt="Drawing of the SVM-45 showing the clamp, adjustable stop, thickness screw, knob and the 45 mm jaw width" />
        <figcaption>SVM-45 design: clamp (1), adjustable stop (2), thickness screw (3), locking knob (4); jaw 45 mm (1-3/4&quot;) wide</figcaption>
      </div>
      <p>The handbook recommends a bench height of 750&ndash;830 mm (29-1/2&ndash;32-2/3&quot;) for knife work with the support vertical, so your forearms are level with the top of the stone; too high a bench makes it hard to keep the stop pressed down.</p>

      <h3>Edge angles</h3>
      <p>Tormek&rsquo;s handbook quotes <em>included</em> angles (both sides together); the KS-123 and most online advice quote angles <em>per side</em>. The table gives both.</p>
      <table class="settings-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Knife</th><th>Included angle</th><th>Per side</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Woodcarving and whittling knives</td><td>20&ndash;25&deg;</td><td>10&ndash;12&deg;</td><td>Keen but fragile; soft woods only at the low end</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Slicers, filleting and paring knives</td><td>25&ndash;30&deg;</td><td>12&ndash;15&deg;</td><td>Thin stock, light duty</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Western chef&rsquo;s, cook&rsquo;s and butcher&rsquo;s knives</td><td>30&ndash;40&deg;</td><td>15&ndash;20&deg;</td><td>15&deg; per side for a good stainless or carbon chef&rsquo;s knife, 17&ndash;20&deg; for softer steel or heavy use</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hunting and outdoor knives</td><td>30&ndash;40&deg;</td><td>15&ndash;20&deg;</td><td>Go toward 20&deg; for batoning or bone</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Japanese double-bevel gyuto/santoku</td><td>24&ndash;30&deg;</td><td>12&ndash;15&deg;</td><td>Hard steel chips at lower angles; fine-graded stone and light pressure</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p55_02.jpg" alt="Tormek chart of recommended included edge angles: 20 to 25 degrees for woodcarving knives, 25 to 30 for slicing, filleting and paring knives, 30 to 40 for butchers', cooks', hunting and sporting knives" />
        <figcaption>Tormek&rsquo;s recommended included edge angles by knife type (HB-10, p. 55)</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Mounting the knife</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Back the large black knob off so the jaws open, then turn the small screw at the hinge end until the jaws match the blade thickness and close parallel. Parallel jaws are what keep the blade from rocking.</li>
        <li>Slide the blade in from the back so the spine sits against the inside of the jaw and the edge stands clear by at least 2 mm (5/64&quot;). Blades narrower than 14 mm (9/16&quot;) at a 25&deg; included angle do not leave enough room; use the SVM-00 for those.</li>
        <li>Position the clamp along the blade. This is the setting that decides what happens at the tip: clamp near the handle and the angle at the tip comes out smaller than at the heel; clamp near the tip and it comes out larger. For a typical 8&quot; chef&rsquo;s knife, centering the jig on the straight part of the edge, roughly a third of the way back from the tip, gives a bevel of even width end to end. Make a mental note (or a pencil tick on the spine) so you can repeat the position next time.</li>
        <li>Check the edge is parallel to the long axis of the jig by sighting along it, then tighten the knob hard.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p53_01.jpg" alt="Three steps: knob loosened, jaws adjusted to blade thickness with the small screw, knob tightened with the knife firmly mounted" />
        <figcaption>Mounting the knife: loosen the knob, fit the jaws to the blade with the small screw, tighten</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p53_02.jpg" alt="Three drawings showing the jig clamped near the handle, correctly positioned, and near the tip, and the effect on the tip angle" />
        <figcaption>Clamp position (a) sets the tip angle: toward the handle makes the tip angle smaller, toward the tip makes it larger; the correct position gives one angle along the whole edge</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p53_03.jpg" alt="Drawing of the minimum 2 mm grip and 14 mm blade width for the SVM-45" />
        <figcaption>Minimum grip 2 mm (5/64&quot;); minimum blade width 14 mm (9/16&quot;) at 25&deg; included. Narrower blades go in the SVM-00</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="note">
        The angle you set applies to the straight part of the blade. On a knife with a lot of belly, the apex at the very tip is further from the pivot and will come out a degree or two steeper unless you lift the handle as you sweep into the curve &mdash; which is exactly the motion described under Grinding.
      </div>

      <h3>Setting the edge angle</h3>
      <p>Put the universal support in the vertical sleeves, drop it lower than you think you need, and leave both locking knobs finger-loose so the Micro Adjust can raise it. Then choose one of three methods:</p>
      <h4>Tormek Marker Method (copying the existing bevel)</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Blacken the whole bevel on one side with a permanent marker.</li>
        <li>Turn the jig stop to its zero mark, rest the jig on the support and, with the machine <strong>off</strong>, set the support so the heel of the bevel just touches the stone.</li>
        <li>Rotate the stone backwards by hand a quarter turn and look at the bevel. Ink gone only at the heel: the angle is too low, raise the support with the Micro Adjust. Ink gone only at the apex: too steep, lower it. Repeat until the whole bevel wipes clean in one stroke.</li>
        <li>Lock the support knobs. The same setting is used after flipping the jig, provided the factory bevels were symmetrical.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p41_marker.jpg" alt="The Marker Method: colouring the bevel with a marker, rotating the stone by hand, and checking where the colour has been removed" />
        <figcaption>The Marker Method (HB-10, p. 41): ink the bevel, turn the stone by hand, adjust until the ink is removed from tip to heel</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p54_01.jpg" alt="Inking the bevel, turning the jig stop to zero, and the earlier stop design with a locking screw" />
        <figcaption>Ink the bevel and turn the stop to zero. Earlier jigs have a locking screw on the stop; the current stop is self-locking</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p54_02.jpg" alt="Setting the universal support so the heel of the bevel touches the stone, then raising it with the Micro Adjust until the whole bevel touches" />
        <figcaption>Set the support so the heel of the bevel touches, then raise it with the Micro Adjust until the whole bevel contacts the stone; check by turning the wheel by hand</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h4>WM-200 AngleMaster (setting a new angle)</h4>
      <p>Set the AngleMaster to the stone diameter and the desired <em>included</em> angle, place it on the stone and adjust the support until the whole bevel lies flat against the setter&rsquo;s blade. On a thin knife the bevel is too short to register, so rest the setter against the flat of the blade instead and set the AngleMaster to <em>half</em> the desired included angle. The AngleMaster&rsquo;s notched edge also measures an existing edge angle. Details of the WM-200 are on the T-8 page.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p54_03.jpg" alt="Setting a 25 degree edge angle with the AngleMaster against the bevel, and measuring an edge in the AngleMaster's grooves" />
        <figcaption>Setting a new angle with the WM-200, and measuring an existing one in its grooves</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p55_01.jpg" alt="Thin knife: the AngleMaster rests on the flat of the blade and is set to half the desired included angle" />
        <figcaption>Thin knives: rest the setter on the blade, not the bevel, and set it to half the included angle (20&deg; on the setter for a 40&deg; edge)</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h4>KS-123 Knife Angle Setter (setting a per-side angle precisely)</h4>
      <p>The fastest and most repeatable option: clip it onto the support, set its protrusion to match the knife in the jig, press its three legs to the stone and raise the support until the pointer reads the per-side angle you want. The full procedure is in the <strong>KS-123</strong> section below.</p>

      <h3>Grinding</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Grade the stone: coarse (the rough side of the SP-650) for a damaged edge or a new angle, fine for a routine resharpening. Fill the trough; the stone must be running wet.</li>
        <li>Switch on. Hold the jig shaft with one hand so the thumb or palm presses the stop down against the back of the support bar, and the handle of the knife in the other. The stop must stay in contact with the support the whole time; if it lifts, the angle changes and the edge can bite into the stone.</li>
        <li>Lower the heel of the edge onto the stone and draw the blade slowly sideways so the whole width of the stone is used. Watch the water: it should sheet evenly over the entire bevel, which tells you the full width of the stone is in contact.</li>
        <li>As you reach the belly, lift the handle and pivot the jig around the stop so the edge stays tangent to the stone all the way to the tip. Then sweep back. Light pressure; the stone does the work.</li>
        <li>Every few passes, stop and feel the <em>far</em> side of the edge with a fingertip moving from the spine across the edge. You are looking for a continuous burr from heel to tip. The last places to develop one are usually the heel and the extreme tip; concentrate the passes there, do not keep grinding the middle.</li>
        <li>With a full-length burr, flip the jig over (knife still clamped) and grind the second side the same way until the burr has moved to the first side. Match the number of passes.</li>
        <li>If you started coarse, re-grade fine and repeat a few light passes per side. Finish with one very light pass on the first side to weaken the burr, then go to the leather wheel.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p56_01.jpg" alt="Holding the knife and jig with the thumb pressing the stop against the support, moving the blade across the stone, raising the handle at the tip, and stroking the back of the blade to feel the burr" />
        <figcaption>Grinding the first side: thumb on the jig pressing the stop to the support, full width of the stone, handle raised at the tip; feel for the burr from the back</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p56_02.jpg" alt="Drawing of a knife edge with the burr showing as a silver line along the whole edge" />
        <figcaption>In good light the burr shows as a silver line; when it runs the whole length, the first side is done</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p56_03.jpg" alt="Jig turned upside down with the knife still clamped to grind the second side, then a light pass on the first side to weaken the burr" />
        <figcaption>Flip the jig with the knife still clamped and grind the second side as much as the first; a light final pass on the first side weakens the burr. Remove the honing wheel for long knives</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svm00_p05_02.jpg" alt="Hand pressing the knife jig stop down against the universal support while grinding toward the edge" />
        <figcaption>The one rule that matters: keep the stop pressed firmly onto the support bar throughout the stroke</figcaption>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Long blades:</strong> if the tip of a 10&quot; chef&rsquo;s knife or slicer starts clipping the honing wheel on the other side of the machine, unscrew and remove the leather wheel for the grinding step.</p>

      <h3>Grinding away from the edge (short knives)</h3>
      <p>Everything above is with the support vertical and the wheel running into the edge, which works for every knife. A knife short enough that the jig can be clamped within about 50 mm (2&quot;) of the tip can alternatively be ground with the support in the horizontal sleeves and the wheel running away from the edge. Grinding pressure is lower, which suits a knife that only needs its edge refreshed, and the knife can stay in the jig to be honed at a controlled angle: just move the support to the honing-wheel side and set it so the leather meets the whole bevel. Long knives cannot be ground this way because the jig fouls the leg of the support.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p57_01.jpg" alt="Short knife clamped within 50 mm of the tip ground away from the edge with the support horizontal, then the support moved to the honing wheel side" />
        <figcaption>Short knives (jig within 50 mm of the tip) can be ground edge trailing and then honed in the jig on the leather wheel</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Honing</h3>
      <p>Turn the machine so the wheels run away from you with the honing wheel on the left. Take the knife out of the jig and hone both sides freehand on the LA-220 leather wheel as described in <strong>Honing Knives</strong> below, laying the whole bevel on the leather so you hone at the angle you ground, a few strokes per side until the burr is gone. Draw the edge through end-grain wood to confirm the burr is completely off. Always hone in the direction of rotation, away from the edge. For short knives ground edge-trailing you can keep the knife in the jig and hone at a controlled angle by moving the support to the honing-wheel side.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p57_02.jpg" alt="Honing both bevels of a knife on the leather wheel with the whole bevel touching, then drawing the edge through end grain to remove the burr" />
        <figcaption>Honing: whole bevel on the leather, alternate sides until the burr disappears; end grain confirms it is gone</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Carver&rsquo;s drawknives</h3>
      <p>A carving drawknife clamps in the SVM-45 like a wide, straight knife, with the handles standing up out of the way. Press the jig down so the stop never leaves the universal support and traverse so the grind runs across the whole width of the stone. Honing is freehand: hold the drawknife diagonally so the handles clear the grinding wheel and alternate bevel and back on the leather until the burr is gone.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p58_01.jpg" alt="Drawknife in the SVM-45 pressed down onto the support, then honed freehand held diagonally on the leather wheel" />
        <figcaption>Carver&rsquo;s drawknife: grind in the jig with the stop pressed down, hone freehand held diagonally, bevel and back alternately</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Typical mistakes</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Grinding until the middle of the edge has a burr and calling it done; the heel and tip still have the old dull apex. Check the whole length.</li>
        <li>Letting the stop float off the support bar, which rounds the bevel and can gouge the stone.</li>
        <li>Clamping too close to the tip, which steepens the tip angle, or re-clamping in a different spot on the second side.</li>
        <li>Pressing hard. Pressure flexes the blade, heats nothing (it is wet) but produces a huge wire edge that is hard to remove and breaks off leaving a dull apex.</li>
        <li>Skipping the fine grade before honing: the leather wheel cannot remove coarse-stone scratches, only polish them.</li>
      </ul>
