      <h2>Pocket, EDC and Hunting Knives &mdash; SVM-45</h2>
      <p>Shorter, thicker blades sharpen the same way as a chef&rsquo;s knife, but three things change: folding knives must be ground edge leading, hunting knives want a stouter angle, and the blade shapes (clip points, recurves, convex grinds) ask for some extra handwork.</p>
      <h3>Setup differences</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Folding knives: vertical support, edge leading, always.</strong> A folder cannot be locked open against the direction of grinding, and the trailing-edge position would push the blade toward closing on your fingers. With the wheel running into the edge the blade is pushed open instead.</li>
        <li><strong>Clamp position.</strong> A 3&quot; blade gives you little choice: center the jig on the straight section just ahead of the ricasso. If the blade is narrower than 14 mm or shorter than 60 mm, move to the SVM-00 holder.</li>
        <li><strong>Angle.</strong> 15&deg; per side for a thin EDC slicer; 17&ndash;20&deg; per side for a hunting knife, skinner or anything that will see bone, batoning or hard use. The KS-123 makes it trivial to pick 17&deg; or 18&deg; and repeat it next time.</li>
        <li><strong>Stone grade.</strong> A touch-up on a hard-steel folder (S30V, M390 and friends) only needs the fine grade and light pressure; those steels burr stubbornly, so plan on more alternating light passes at the end and a longer session on the leather wheel.</li>
        <li><strong>Fixed blades with a guard.</strong> If the finger guard fouls the jig, clamp further toward the tip and accept that the heel gets a slightly lower angle; or grind the last few millimeters at the heel with the handle lifted.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Blade shapes</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Drop and clip points</strong> are just a chef&rsquo;s-knife belly in miniature: lift the handle sooner and more.</li>
        <li><strong>Recurves.</strong> A 250 mm stone cannot reach into a concave section of edge, and if you push the recurve flat onto the stone you grind the two humps on either side instead. Sharpen the straight and convex parts normally, then address the recurve on the rounded corner of the stone or, if the recurve is slight, let the leather wheel&rsquo;s give do the work. Tormek&rsquo;s recurve video below shows the full approach.</li>
        <li><strong>Convex edges.</strong> The jig grinds a flat bevel. To keep a convex edge, grind a primary bevel at the lower angle, then raise the support slightly and take a few light passes at a steeper angle to establish the apex, and blend the facets on the leather wheel. Repeating this at every sharpening maintains a working convex without a slack belt.</li>
        <li><strong>Serrations and gut hooks</strong> do not go in the jig at all; see the gut-hook video in the Videos section.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="note">
        Scandi-ground bushcraft knives have a single wide bevel with no secondary edge. Do not clamp them in the SVM-45 and grind a micro-bevel on top unless that is what you want; they are normally laid flat on the bevel and honed, which on a Tormek means the SVM-45 with the Marker Method so the whole bevel wipes clean, then the leather wheel.
      </div>
