      <h2>Long, Thin, Flexible Knives &mdash; SVM-140</h2>
      <p>The SVM-140 is the SVM-45 with a 140 mm (5-1/2&quot;) wide jaw. Everything else &mdash; the shaft, the stop, the angle-setting methods, the grinding motion &mdash; is identical, so this section only covers what the wide jaw changes. It needs a blade at least 160 mm (6-1/4&quot;) long and is used with the support vertical, grinding toward the edge. Like the SVM-45 it has no current leaflet; the <em>Long Knife Jig SVM-140</em> chapter of the HB-10 handbook (page 62) is the source here.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p62_01.jpg" alt="SVM-140 long knife jig holding a filleting knife on a Tormek, with a drawing showing the 160 mm minimum blade length" />
        <figcaption>SVM-140 in use on a filleting knife; minimum blade length (a) 160 mm (6-1/4&quot;)</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/hb10_p62_02.jpg" alt="Drawing of the SVM-140 showing the 140 mm wide clamp, adjustable stop, thickness screw and knob" />
        <figcaption>SVM-140 design: clamp (1) 140 mm (5-1/2&quot;) wide, adjustable stop (2), thickness screw (3), locking knob (4)</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>Why the wide jaw matters</h3>
      <p>A filleting knife or a long carving slicer is thin enough to bend under its own grinding pressure. Clamped in a 45 mm jaw, the unsupported blade on either side of the clamp flexes away from the stone as you press, springs back as you ease off, and the result is an edge angle that wanders along the length and a bevel that is visibly rounded over near the tip. The 140 mm jaw stiffens the middle 5-1/2&quot; of the blade, so what you set is what you grind. It also stops the heel and tip from wagging as you sweep across the stone.</p>
      <h3>Technique</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Center the jaw on the blade so an equal length sticks out at each end. On a 9&quot; slicer that leaves under two inches free at either end.</li>
        <li>Set the angle low: 12&ndash;15&deg; per side (25&ndash;30&deg; included) for a filleting knife, 15&deg; for a slicer. The KS-123 works with the SVM-140; set its protrusion with the knife in the jig as usual.</li>
        <li>Grade the stone fine unless the edge is damaged. A flexible blade removes metal fast enough on the fine grade and the coarse grade raises a wire edge that is hard to control.</li>
        <li>Grind with the lightest pressure that still produces a burr, and put your supporting fingers on the blade close to the edge, directly over the contact with the stone, rather than on the handle. That takes the flex out of the free ends. Keep the jig stop pressed to the support.</li>
        <li>Expect the burr to appear in the middle first; finish the heel and tip with the stroke slowed down at each end rather than with more pressure.</li>
        <li>Remove the honing wheel if the tip reaches across to it.</li>
        <li>Hone freehand on the leather wheel with the same light touch; a flexible blade will bend into the leather if you lean on it and round the apex over.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="note">
        Boning knives are usually stiff enough and short enough for the SVM-45; the SVM-140 is for blades that visibly flex when you push the tip against the bench.
      </div>
      <div class="video-row">
        <div class="video">
          <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/z4-HArUSCJo" title="Sharpen thin and flexible knives with the Tormek Knife Jig SVM-140" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
          <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>Sharpen thin and flexible knives with the Tormek Knife Jig SVM-140</em> (1:49)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="video">
          <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/ucPqOQcZAj8" title="How to Sharpen a Filleting Knife on Tormek T-1, T-2 or T-8" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
          <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>How to Sharpen a Filleting Knife on Tormek T-1, T-2 or T-8</em> (22:41)</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Small Knives &mdash; SVM-00 Small Knife Holder</h2>
      <p>The SVM-00 solves the problem of blades that are too short or too narrow for the SVM-45 jaws to grip without the jaws touching the stone: paring knives, sloyd and whittling knives, chip-carving and detail knives, skews, and small pocket knives. Instead of clamping the blade, it clamps the <em>handle</em> and presents a flat steel guide bar that the SVM-45 jaws grip in place of a blade. You then grind exactly as with the SVM-45.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svm00_p03_01.jpg" alt="SVM-00 small knife holder mounted in the SVM-45 knife jig on a Tormek, with silhouettes of the carving, skew and pocket knives it suits" />
        <figcaption>The SVM-00 in use on the T-8, and the kinds of knives it is meant for</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>Design</h3>
      <p>Guide bar (1), handle grip (2) and adjustable clamp (3), with a knob (4) that tightens the clamp onto the knife handle. The clamp faces are shaped to hold a wooden or plastic handle without marking it. The edge angle is set as usual with the SVM-45 stop (6) and the Micro Adjust.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svm00_p03_02.jpg" alt="Drawing of the SVM-00 small knife holder with guide bar, handle grip, clamp and knob numbered, above the SVM-45 jig with its adjustable stop" />
        <figcaption>SVM-00: guide bar (1), handle grip (2), adjustable clamp (3), knob (4); the SVM-45 below with its adjustable stop (6)</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/web_svm00.jpg" alt="SVM-00 small knife holder with a small carving knife clamped by its handle" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/web_svm00_use.jpg" alt="SVM-00 in the SVM-45 jig on a Tormek grinding a small knife" />
      </div>
      <h3>Mounting the knife</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Lay the holder flat on the bench with the guide bar and the knife blade both overhanging the edge of the bench, so the two are pressed flat against the same surface. This is what aligns the edge with the guide bar.</li>
        <li>Slide the handle into the clamp. How deep depends on the handle shape; a tapered sloyd handle may need to go in further than a flat pocket-knife handle. It must be held firmly without rocking.</li>
        <li>Holding bar and blade down on the bench, tighten the knob.</li>
        <li>Sight along the guide bar: the blade should lie in line with it, not canted up or down.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svm00_p04_01.jpg" alt="Three steps: holder on a table edge, pressing guide bar and blade flat against the table, checking the blade is in line with the guide bar" />
        <figcaption>Mounting the knife in the holder on a table edge</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svm00_p04_02.jpg" alt="Two knives mounted at different depths in the SVM-00 depending on handle shape" />
        <figcaption>Handle shape decides how far into the holder the knife goes</figcaption>
      </div>
      <ol start="5">
        <li>Open the SVM-45 (knob loose), fit the guide bar into its jaws and close the jaws onto it with the small adjusting screw, then tighten the large black knob.</li>
        <li>Check that the knife edge is parallel to the SVM-45. If it is skewed, the two sides will come out at different angles.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svm00_p04_03.jpg" alt="Three steps: SVM-45 knob loosened, guide bar fitted with the adjusting screw, checking the edge is parallel to the jig" />
        <figcaption>Mounting the SVM-00 in the SVM-45</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svm00_p04_04.jpg" alt="Wrong mounting with the edge angled to the jig, and correct mounting with the edge parallel" />
        <figcaption>Wrong (edge not parallel to the SVM-45) and right</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>Sharpening and honing</h3>
      <p>Angles for these knives are low: 10&ndash;12&deg; per side for a carving or sloyd knife used in basswood or green wood, 12&ndash;15&deg; for a paring knife or a pocket knife. Set the angle with the Marker Method (these knives usually have a wide, flat bevel that is easy to ink and read), the WM-200 or the KS-123 (which is compatible with the SVM-00 in the SVM-45). Then follow the general knife workflow. The small blade means a burr forms in seconds on the coarse grade; use the fine grade for everything except repairing chips.</p>
      <p><strong>Direction:</strong> vertical support, edge leading, is the normal position and is required for folding knives. Horizontal, edge trailing, is slower and gentler and suits a short carving knife you only want to refresh; it also lets you hone in the jig at a controlled angle. Whichever you use, press the SVM-45 stop against the support the whole time.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svm00_p05_04.jpg" alt="Note about knives that cannot be mounted in line with the guide bar, with a drawing of a skewed blade" />
        <figcaption>If a knife cannot be mounted parallel, or has an asymmetric grind, set each side separately with the Micro Adjust</figcaption>
      </div>
      <p>Carving knives are usually honed with the whole bevel flat on the leather, which means the support in the horizontal position with the knife still in the jig, set by the Marker Method so the leather touches heel and apex together. Keep the pressure very light: the leather compresses and a heavy hand will convex the bevel.</p>
      <div class="video">
        <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/Of6KqiwaCP4" title="Sharpen small knives with the Tormek Small Knife Holder SVM-00" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
        <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>Sharpen small knives with the Tormek Small Knife Holder SVM-00</em> (1:49)</p>
      </div>

      <h2>KS-123 Knife Angle Setter</h2>
      <p>The KS-123 replaces the Marker Method and the AngleMaster for knives. The problem it solves: the edge angle on a Tormek depends on three things at once &mdash; the support height, how far the knife sticks out of the jig, and the current diameter of the stone, which shrinks every time you true it. The KS-123 measures all three physically. You set its protrusion to match the knife in its jig, rest its three reference legs on the stone, and the pointer reads the per-side angle directly. Because the legs touch the actual stone, it needs no diameter correction. It works with the SVM-45, SVM-140 and SVM-00 (in the SVM-45), as well as the newer KJ-45 and KJ-140.</p>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/web_ks123.jpg" alt="Tormek KS-123 knife angle setter product photo" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/web_ks123_scale.jpg" alt="Close-up of the KS-123 angle scale and indicator" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/web_ks123_angle.jpg" alt="KS-123 on the universal support with a knife in the jig, setting the angle" />
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/ks123_p04_01.jpg" alt="Labelled drawing of the KS-123: retractable part, holder for the universal support, angle scale, indicator, three reference legs, V-shaped edge rest and locking knob" />
        <figcaption>KS-123 parts: (1) retractable part, (2) holder for the universal support, (3) angle scale in degrees per side, (4) angle indicator, (5) three reference legs, (6) V-shaped edge rest, (7) protrusion locking knob</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="warning">
        The machine stays <strong>off</strong> for the entire setting process. You will be sliding a knife edge within millimeters of the stone; do not let it touch.
      </div>
      <h3>Preparation</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Clamp the knife in its jig as usual and set it aside.</li>
        <li>Loosen the KS-123&rsquo;s protrusion locking knob (7) and both locking knobs on the universal support.</li>
        <li>Fit the support in the vertical sleeves and set it a little <em>lower</em> than you expect to need. Raising it with the Micro Adjust is the adjustment you will make; it is awkward to go the other way.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/ks123_p04_02.jpg" alt="Preparation steps: knife mounted in the jig, locking knobs loosened, universal support lowered" />
        <figcaption>Preparation: knife in the jig, knobs loose, support set low</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>1. Mount the setter</h3>
      <p>Snap the holder (2) onto the universal support bar; it clicks on. The three legs (5) should hang over the stone but do not yet need to touch it.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/ks123_p05_01.jpg" alt="Clipping the KS-123 onto the universal support, with its legs over the grinding wheel" />
        <figcaption>Clip the KS-123 onto the support; legs over the stone, machine off</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>2. Match the protrusion to your knife</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>With the knife jig on the <em>right</em> of the setter, rest the knife edge centered in the V-shaped edge rest (6).</li>
        <li>Slide the knife forward; the retractable part (1) moves with it. Keep going until the knife jig&rsquo;s stop sits down on the universal support exactly as it would when grinding, flat face against the bar.</li>
        <li>Tighten the locking knob (7) so the retractable part stays where it is. The setter is now a copy of your knife&rsquo;s geometry: edge-to-support distance included.</li>
        <li>Lift the knife and jig away and set them aside.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/ks123_p05_02.jpg" alt="Knife edge resting in the V-shaped rest with the jig stop against the support, locking the retractable part" />
        <figcaption>Setting the protrusion: edge in the V-rest, jig stop down on the support, lock knob (7)</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="note">
        If you grind away a lot of steel &mdash; repairing a chip, reprofiling &mdash; the edge moves closer to the support and the angle creeps up. Redo step 2 before the final fine passes.
      </div>
      <h3>3. Set the angle</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Put a fingertip on each of the three legs and press lightly so all three sit on the stone.</li>
        <li>Turn the Micro Adjust to raise the support until the indicator (4) points at the per-side angle you want on the scale (3). Keep all three legs in contact while you do it.</li>
        <li>Lock both universal support knobs, unclip the KS-123, and put the knife jig back on the support. Grind.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/ks123_p06_01.jpg" alt="Three steps: fingers on the three legs, raising the universal support until the indicator reads the angle, locking the support" />
        <figcaption>Setting the angle: legs on the stone, raise the support to the mark, lock and remove the setter</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/ks123_p06_02.jpg" alt="If the support is too low the legs cannot all touch the stone; raise it until the two outer legs rest on the wheel and start again" />
        <figcaption>If the support started too low, the three legs cannot all touch at once: raise the support until the two outer legs sit on the stone, then redo step 3</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>Notes on using it</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>The scale reads <strong>degrees per side</strong>. For a Tormek handbook recommendation of 30&ndash;40&deg; included, set 15&ndash;20&deg;.</li>
        <li>The setting is valid for both sides of a symmetric knife. Flip the jig and keep the support where it is.</li>
        <li>With the SVM-00, the &ldquo;knife jig&rdquo; in step 2 is the SVM-45 holding the small knife holder; the procedure is otherwise identical.</li>
        <li>Write the angle and the clamp position on a card for each knife you sharpen regularly; with the KS-123 the next session is a two-minute job.</li>
        <li>It is for the grinding wheel only. For the honing angle use the Marker Method on the leather wheel, or hone freehand.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Scissors and Shears &mdash; SVX-150</h2>
      <p>Scissors do not cut like a knife. Each blade has a single steep bevel, often around 60&deg;, and a flat (sometimes very slightly hollowed) inside face; the two inside faces rub together and shear the material. Sharpening means restoring the bevel only. The inside face must stay flat and untouched, because that is what sets the fit between the blades.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svx150_p01_01.jpg" alt="Scissors jig in use on the Tormek, with drawings of scissors, garden shears and a planer blade" />
        <figcaption>SVX-150 in use, and what it sharpens: scissors, garden shears and HSS portable planer blades</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>Design</h3>
      <p>The jig is two parts. A support plate (1) slides onto the universal support and provides a low-friction table; a holder (2) with two clamps (3) grips the scissor blade and slides on the plate. Each clamp is opened to the blade thickness with a setting screw (4) and locked with a large knob (5). For ordinary scissors you use only the right-hand clamp; for long blades and garden shears you use both.</p>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svx150_p01_02.jpg" alt="Drawing of the SVX-150 with support plate, holder, clamps, setting screw and knob numbered" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/web_svx150.jpg" alt="Tormek SVX-150 scissors jig product photo" />
      </div>
      <h3>Setup and edge angle</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Universal support in the <strong>horizontal</strong> sleeves on the grinding-wheel side; the wheel runs toward the edge. Slide the support plate onto the bar and lock the support as low as it will go while the plate still clears the stone.</li>
        <li>Open the right clamp with the setting screw to a hair more than the blade thickness, loosen the big knob, and slide the scissor blade in, bevel up, with about 3 mm (1/8&quot;) of the blade protruding past the clamp. Check that the <em>other</em> blade hangs clear of the side of the stone, then tighten the knob.</li>
        <li>To <strong>keep the existing angle</strong>, ink the bevel, set the holder on the plate, roll the stone back by hand and adjust the support height until the ink wipes off the whole bevel. To <strong>set a new angle</strong>, rest the WM-200 so its indicator touches the holder between the two clamps and set the support for the angle you want; 60&deg; is normal for household and tailor&rsquo;s scissors, 55&deg; for fine paper scissors, 65&ndash;70&deg; for heavy shears.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svx150_p02_01.jpg" alt="Sliding the support plate onto the universal support, inking a scissor bevel, and setting the angle with the AngleMaster against the holder" />
        <figcaption>Setting the edge angle: support plate on, Marker Method or AngleMaster</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svx150_p02_02.jpg" alt="Loosening the knob, adjusting the clamp with the setting screw to the blade thickness, and tightening with the second blade clear of the stone" />
        <figcaption>Mounting a scissor blade: 3 mm protruding, other blade clear of the wheel</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>Grinding</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Grade the stone fine. Scissors need very little metal removed and the coarse grade raises a burr that bends over onto the inside face.</li>
        <li>Rest the holder on the plate with the bevel on the stone and a light downward pressure, and slide it back and forth across the stone following the curve of the blade from pivot to tip. The plate&rsquo;s sliding surface lets the holder move freely while the plate holds the angle.</li>
        <li>Grind until a fine, even burr has formed along the full length on the inside face and the bevel shows one uniform fresh surface from pivot to tip.</li>
        <li>Loosen the knob, swap in the other blade and repeat.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svx150_p02_03.jpg" alt="Grinding a scissor blade on the support plate, moving across the wheel along the blade, and checking for a burr" />
        <figcaption>Grinding: light pressure, follow the blade, stop at a full-length burr</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svx150_p03_01.jpg" alt="Removing the first blade, mounting the second, and grinding it the same way" />
        <figcaption>Second blade</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="danger">
        <strong>Never grind the inside (flat) face of a scissor blade on the wheel.</strong> Removing even a little from it changes the set and the blades will fold the cloth instead of cutting it. The only thing that touches the inside face is the end-grain deburring below.
      </div>
      <h3>Deburring (no honing)</h3>
      <p>Scissors are not honed. A slightly toothy bevel grips the material so it does not slide ahead of the cut, which is why freshly ground scissors on the fine-graded stone cut better than polished ones. Remove the burr by closing the scissors a few times on a scrap of softwood end grain, or by drawing each blade&rsquo;s inside face flat across the wood. Then open and close them: they should close smoothly without a gritty feel and cut a thin tissue cleanly at the very tip.</p>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svx150_p03_03.jpg" alt="Drawing a scissor blade through a block of end grain to strip the burr" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/web_svx150_tip.jpg" alt="Checking the sharpened tip of a pair of scissors" />
      </div>
      <h3>Garden shears and long blades</h3>
      <p>Use both clamps so a long blade cannot pivot in the holder, and set the angle with the AngleMaster between the clamps. Pruning shears and hedge shears are coarser tools; the standard grade of the stone is fine for them and the angle can be whatever the factory put on. Bypass pruners are ground on the outside bevel of the cutting blade only; the anvil or hook blade is left alone.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svx150_p03_02.jpg" alt="Garden shears mounted using both clamps of the SVX-150" />
        <figcaption>Larger scissors and shears: both clamps</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>Special cases</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pinking shears.</strong> The zig-zag is cut into the blade; the bevel still runs as one continuous face along the teeth. They can be ground in the jig with one or two very light passes, and the inside faces must not be touched or the teeth will stop meshing. If the teeth are damaged rather than dull, that is a specialist job.</li>
        <li><strong>Hairdressing and grooming shears.</strong> German-style shears with a plain bevelled edge can be done like household scissors at their existing angle. Japanese-style convex-edge shears, and anything with a hollow-ground inside and a hone line, should not go in this jig: the SVX-150 grinds a flat bevel and will destroy the convex edge. Send them to a shear specialist.</li>
        <li><strong>Serrated (micro-serrated) scissors.</strong> Grind only the plain blade; the serrated blade is left alone.</li>
        <li><strong>Portable electric planer blades (HSS only).</strong> Clamp like a scissor blade and grind the bevel; unlike scissors these <em>are</em> honed. Keep the blade in the holder and alternate bevel and back on the leather wheel until the burr is gone. Carbide blades cannot be ground on this stone.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Typical mistakes</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Grinding the inside face &ldquo;to clean it up.&rdquo;</li>
        <li>Letting the second blade touch the side of the stone while the first is being ground.</li>
        <li>Too much pressure, which rolls a heavy burr onto the inside face.</li>
        <li>Honing scissors on the leather wheel; polished scissors push cloth instead of cutting it.</li>
        <li>Tightening the pivot screw to compensate for a dull edge; after sharpening, back it off to where the blades close smoothly.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="video">
        <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/IVl8lTiMlAA" title="Sharpen scissors with the Tormek Scissors Jig SVX-150" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
        <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>Sharpen scissors with the Tormek Scissors Jig SVX-150</em> (1:45)</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Axes and Hatchets &mdash; SVA-170</h2>
      <p>The SVA-170 is the simplest jig in the kit: a bent stainless clamp with a rubber-lined wedge stop that slides along the universal support. The axe head sits in the clamp with its edge toward the stone, and the rotation of the stone &mdash; which must run <em>toward</em> the edge &mdash; pushes the head back into the wedge. You hold and steer the axe; the jig holds the angle. It takes edges up to 170 mm (6-3/4&quot;); a full-size felling axe is ground freehand on the vertical support with a finger as a stop.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/sva170_p01_01.jpg" alt="Hatchet being ground in the SVA-170 on a Tormek, with drawings of axes up to 170 mm" />
        <figcaption>SVA-170 in use; axes and hatchets up to 170 mm</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/sva170_p01_02.jpg" alt="Drawing of the SVA-170 clamp with its rubber wedge stop" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/web_sva170.jpg" alt="Tormek SVA-170 axe jig product photo" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/web_sva170_use.jpg" alt="Axe being sharpened in the SVA-170 on a Tormek T-8" />
      </div>
      <h3>Edge angles and bevel shape</h3>
      <table class="settings-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Axe</th><th>Included angle</th><th>Bevel</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Carving axe, carpenter&rsquo;s axe, hatchet for softwood</td><td>25&ndash;30&deg;</td><td>Flat or nearly flat; longer, lower bevel on the side that faces the work for a carving axe</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hatchet for hardwood, limbing</td><td>30&deg;</td><td>Flat with the heels rounded</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Felling axe, hunting axe, splitting hatchet</td><td>30&ndash;40&deg;</td><td>Convex: round off the heels so the edge pops out of the cut</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>The split between carving and chopping axes is the important one. A carving axe is used like a big knife, held close to the surface, and wants a thin flat bevel that registers against the wood; a chopping or splitting axe takes impact and wants a steeper, convex edge that will not chip or stick. A carpenter&rsquo;s axe is often ground asymmetrically, with the longer, lower bevel on the side toward the wood (the right side for a right-hander) so that face acts as a guide.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/sva170_p02_02.jpg" alt="Diagrams of a carpenter's axe with a longer bevel on the wood side, a felling axe at 30 to 40 degrees with rounded heels, and the AngleMaster on an axe" />
        <figcaption>Carpenter&rsquo;s axe with the long bevel toward the wood; felling axe at 30&ndash;40&deg; with rounded heels; measuring with the WM-200</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>Setup</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Universal support in the <strong>vertical</strong> sleeves, grinding toward the edge. This jig does not work edge trailing.</li>
        <li>If the handle will hit the honing wheel as you swing the axe across the stone, unscrew and remove the leather wheel for the session.</li>
        <li>Slide the clamp onto the support bar and seat the axe head in it with the edge toward the stone and the cheek against the rubber wedge.</li>
        <li>Set the angle: ink the bevel and use the Marker Method, or set the WM-200 to the stone diameter and the chosen included angle and adjust the support until the setter lies on the bevel.</li>
        <li>Grade the stone coarse for a neglected or nicked axe, fine for a touch-up on a carving axe. Keep the trough full; an axe head pulls a lot of water off the stone.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3>Grinding</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Switch on. Put your palm on the axe head close to the wheel and press lightly; the stone&rsquo;s rotation will drive the head back into the wedge stop and the grind self-stabilizes.</li>
        <li>Follow the edge. Most axe edges are curved; raise and lower the handle to keep the part of the edge on the stone tangent to it, from heel to toe and back.</li>
        <li>Let the clamp slide sideways on the support as you go so the whole width of the stone is used and it wears evenly.</li>
        <li>Grind until a burr has formed along the whole edge on the far side, then take the axe out, turn it over, seat it again and grind the second face the same way. For an asymmetric carpenter&rsquo;s axe, reset the support for the second face (lower for the long bevel, higher for the short one) and confirm with the marker.</li>
        <li>For a convex edge: after the primary bevel is ground, lower the universal support a step and grind the heel of the bevel away, then a second step if needed. Grinding in two or three flats and blending them on the leather wheel gives a smooth convex.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/sva170_p02_01.jpg" alt="Placing the axe in the jig, pressing with the palm close to the wheel, and following the curved edge by raising and lowering the handle" />
        <figcaption>Grinding: palm pressure close to the wheel, follow the curve, slide the jig across the stone</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="warning">
        An axe head is heavy and the edge is leading into the stone. Keep both hands on the axe, keep the edge from catching on the side of the stone when you enter and leave the wheel, and never reach across the wheel while it runs. Clear the honing wheel if there is any chance the handle will clip it.
      </div>
      <h3>Honing</h3>
      <p>Hone both bevels on the leather wheel, <strong>always with the wheel running away from the edge</strong>, freehand, with honing compound on the leather. Polishing the bevels makes an axe cut noticeably easier and the edge lasts longer, because a polished bevel slides through the wood rather than tearing. On a convex edge, roll the head slightly through the stroke to polish the whole curve. Strip any remaining burr by chopping into end grain a few times, then check for a continuous reflection-free apex.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/sva170_p02_03.jpg" alt="Honing an axe bevel on the leather honing wheel, edge trailing" />
        <figcaption>Honing the bevels on the leather wheel, away from the edge</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>Typical mistakes</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Grinding only the middle of a curved edge, leaving the toe and heel dull. Keep the handle moving.</li>
        <li>Forgetting to reset the angle for the second face of an asymmetric axe.</li>
        <li>Thinning a splitting axe to a carving angle; it will chip on the first knot.</li>
        <li>Letting the handle swing into the honing wheel.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="video">
        <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/KXRysXAUwTw" title="Sharpen axes with the Tormek Axe Jig SVA-170" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
        <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>Sharpen axes with the Tormek Axe Jig SVA-170</em> (1:11)</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Short Tools &mdash; SVS-38</h2>
      <p>The SVS-38 holds straight-shanked tools that are too short for the SE-77 square-edge jig: butt chisels, Japanese chisels, carving chisels and gouges, straight V-tools, cutters for reciprocating power carvers, and small plane irons such as block-plane and spokeshave blades. The tool is clamped by a single knob screw in a body with two parallel flanges that straddle the universal support bar. Those flanges are the point of the design: the jig cannot twist on the bar, so even when you roll a gouge the edge stays square to the stone and you can give all your attention to where the stone is cutting.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p01_01.jpg" alt="SVS-38 holding a short carving gouge on a Tormek, with drawings of straight gouges, V-tools, short chisels, square carving chisels and power-carver cutters" />
        <figcaption>SVS-38 in use, and the straight-shanked tools it is designed for</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/web_svs38.jpg" alt="Tormek SVS-38 short tool jig product photo, front view" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/web_svs38_use.jpg" alt="Short chisel clamped in the SVS-38 on the universal support" />
      </div>
      <table class="settings-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Spec</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Maximum tool width</td><td>38 mm (1-1/2&quot;); the older SVS-32 took 32 mm</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Minimum tool length, single bevel</td><td>45 mm (1-3/4&quot;) at a 20&deg; edge angle</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Minimum tool length, square (double-bevel) carving chisel</td><td>about 60 mm (2-3/8&quot;) at 25&deg;</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Smallest gouge</td><td>about 3 mm (1/8&quot;)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Universal support</td><td>Horizontal, grinding away from the edge</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p01_02.jpg" alt="Dimension drawings of the SVS-38 showing the 38 mm maximum width and 45 mm minimum tool length" />
        <figcaption>Capacity: 38 mm wide, tools as short as 45 mm</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>How it differs from the SE-77</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>The SE-77 registers the tool&rsquo;s long flat shank against a wide clamp and rides the support on a single slot; it needs a few inches of blade to clamp and can be adjusted for camber and squareness. The SVS-38 clamps a short tool with one screw and keeps square by straddling the bar with its flanges; there is no camber adjustment.</li>
        <li>The SVS-38 can be <em>rotated</em> on the bar without losing squareness, which is what makes it usable for gouges and V-tools. The SE-77 is for flat edges only.</li>
        <li>The SVS-38 cannot be turned upside down the way the SVS-50 multi jig can. For a tool with symmetrical bevels you unclamp, turn the tool 180&deg; and re-clamp.</li>
        <li>Use the SE-77 whenever the tool is long enough; it gives a wider reference and easier squareness. Use the SVS-38 when it is not, or when the tool is a gouge.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Short chisels, Japanese chisels and small plane irons</h3>
      <p>A straight single bevel is the easy case. Angles: 25&deg; is the general-purpose bench-chisel angle; drop to 20&deg; for paring in soft wood; go to 30&deg; or more for mallet work in hard wood. Japanese chisels (nomi) are normally 27&ndash;30&deg;, and their hard laminated edge chips if taken thinner. Block-plane irons are usually 25&deg; (bevel up with a 12&deg; bed) and spokeshave blades 25&ndash;30&deg;.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p06_02.jpg" alt="Diagram of chisel edge angles of 20, 25 and 30 degrees" />
        <figcaption>Chisel edge angles: 20&deg; delicate paring, 25&deg; general, 30&deg; mallet work in hardwood</figcaption>
      </div>
      <ol>
        <li>Check the edge is square to the sides. If not, square it first with the tool resting directly on the horizontal support, held close to the stone, before it goes in the jig.</li>
        <li>On a new chisel check the back is flat. If it has grinding marks or a belly, flatten it on the side of the stone (the T-8 page covers this) and polish it on the leather wheel. Do <strong>not</strong> do this to a Japanese chisel&rsquo;s hollow back; the hollow is intentional, and its back is only ever lapped flat on a stone, never ground.</li>
        <li>Clamp the chisel in the jig with the back against the jig body and the bevel facing the stone, square, with the handle touching the jig body if the tool is short enough. Tighten the knob screw firmly.</li>
        <li>Universal support in the <strong>horizontal</strong> sleeves. Set the angle: ink the bevel and use the Marker Method to keep the existing angle, or set the WM-200 to the stone diameter and the angle and adjust the support until the bevel lies on the setter.</li>
        <li>Grade the stone coarse if the edge is chipped or the angle is changing, otherwise fine.</li>
        <li>Press with your thumb on the blade close to the edge, hold the handle with the other hand to keep the edge presented flat and not twisted, and grind. Do <em>not</em> slide the jig sideways while grinding; lift it, move it to a fresh spot on the stone and set it down again. Check often that the bevel is coming in evenly across the width.</li>
        <li>Grind until a burr runs the full width of the back, re-grade fine and take a few light passes, then go to the leather wheel.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p06_01.jpg" alt="Setting the existing edge angle of a chisel in the SVS-38 with the Marker Method, and setting a new one with the AngleMaster" />
        <figcaption>Setting the chisel edge angle: Marker Method or AngleMaster</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p07_01.jpg" alt="Thumb pressed close to the edge of a chisel in the SVS-38 while grinding" />
        <figcaption>Grinding: thumb close to the edge, lift to move across the stone rather than sliding</figcaption>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Honing a chisel:</strong> leave it in the jig, move the support to the honing-wheel side (still horizontal) and set it with the Marker Method so the leather touches the whole bevel. Hone the bevel until the burr is gone and the bevel is mirror-bright, then hone the back freehand, holding the chisel tangent to the wheel so the tip is not rounded.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p07_02.jpg" alt="Honing a chisel bevel in the jig on the leather wheel, then honing the back freehand held tangent to the wheel" />
        <figcaption>Honing: bevel in the jig at the grinding angle; back freehand, tangent to the wheel</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="note">
        Japanese chisels: fine grade only, light pressure, and stop as soon as the burr appears. The hard steel layer is thin and brittle, and a heavy burr will take a flake of it with it when it breaks off. Hone the bevel on the leather; the back gets a few light strokes on a flat stone, not the wheel.
      </div>

      <h3>Square carving chisels (double bevel)</h3>
      <p>These have equal bevels on both faces. Grind one bevel as above, then unclamp, turn the tool 180&deg; and re-clamp with the handle touching the jig body exactly as before &mdash; that repeats the projection so the second bevel comes out identical. Minimum blade length is about 60 mm at 25&deg; because of the re-clamping.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p07_03.jpg" alt="A square carving chisel, and the chisel clamped with its handle touching the jig body so both mountings repeat" />
        <figcaption>Square carving chisel: handle against the jig body at both mountings</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Carving gouges</h3>
      <p>The first question with a small gouge is whether it needs the stone at all. A gouge that still has its shape and angle and has merely lost its bite goes straight to the leather wheel; the leather removes enough steel from a 6 mm gouge to bring the edge back. Grind only when the edge is chipped, the shape is wrong, or you are changing the angle. The sequence below is for that case and uses the <em>line of light</em>: a deliberately blunted edge reflects a bright line, and you grind until that line disappears evenly.</p>
      <h4>Shaping the edge</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Rest the gouge directly on the horizontal support, close to the stone, and shape the edge profile (square across, or with the wings swept back as you prefer; a profile angle of about 20&deg; is a common starting point).</li>
        <li>Blunt the new edge deliberately by drawing it across the fine side of the SP-650 stone grader. You now have a uniform bright line along the edge that tells you exactly where steel still needs to come off.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p02_01.jpg" alt="Shaping a gouge edge on the universal support, the edge plane angle of about 20 degrees, and blunting the edge on the stone grader" />
        <figcaption>Shaping: profile the edge on the support, then blunt it on the fine side of the stone grader to create a line of light</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p02_02.jpg" alt="Gouge edge showing a bright line of light along the blunted edge" />
        <figcaption>The line of light is your guide to where to grind</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h4>Edge angle and grinding</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Clamp the gouge in the jig, flute up or down according to which face carries the bevel (outside for ordinary gouges). Set the angle with the Marker Method to keep what it has, or by eye / with the WM-200 for a new angle. Carving gouges run 20&ndash;25&deg; for softwood and relief work, 25&ndash;30&deg; for hardwood and mallet work; a very low angle on a short gouge is fragile.</li>
        <li>Grind where the line of light is thickest, rolling the jig on the support so the whole curve of the edge passes over the stone. Check constantly; it is very easy to over-grind one spot of a small tool.</li>
        <li>Grade the stone fine as the line thins out. Take the burr off freehand on the leather wheel (tool still in the jig) so you can see the real line of light and not the burr. Keep alternating light grinding and burr removal.</li>
        <li>The instant the line of light vanishes along the whole edge, stop. The edge is sharp. Be especially careful at the corners of the gouge, where it is easy to round the wings over.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p03_01.jpg" alt="Marker Method on a gouge bevel, and setting a new angle by eye or with the AngleMaster" />
        <figcaption>Setting the gouge edge angle</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p03_02.jpg" alt="Grinding where the line of light is thickest while rolling the gouge, and grading the stone fine" />
        <figcaption>Grind where the line is thickest, rolling the tool; grade fine as it thins</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p04_01.jpg" alt="Continue grinding, remove the burr freehand on the leather wheel with the tool in the jig, grind again with light pressure" />
        <figcaption>Alternate light grinding with freehand burr removal so you can read the line of light</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p04_02.jpg" alt="Stop when the line of light disappears; be careful not to round the corners of the gouge" />
        <figcaption>Stop when the line of light is gone; watch the corners</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="warning">
        The burr looks like a line of light. Strip it on the leather wheel often during the final passes or you will keep grinding a sharp edge thinking it is still blunt.
      </div>
      <h4>Honing a gouge</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Ink the bevel, keep the tool in the jig, and move the support to the honing-wheel side. Set it so the leather touches the whole bevel: if the ink comes off only at the heel, move the support away from the wheel; only at the tip, move it toward the wheel. Check with the wheel running.</li>
        <li>Charge both the flat leather wheel and the profiled LA-120 wheel (if fitted) with honing compound.</li>
        <li>Hone the outside bevel, rolling the jig on the support with pressure near the edge, and move the jig sideways so the leather wears evenly.</li>
        <li>Take the gouge out of the jig and hone the inside of the flute freehand on the profiled leather wheel, or on a slip, until the burr is gone.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p04_03.jpg" alt="Inking the bevel and setting the honing angle; wrong setting with the wheel hitting the heel" />
        <figcaption>Setting the honing angle with the Marker Method; wrong (heel contact only)</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p05_01.jpg" alt="Wrong setting hitting the tip, correct setting touching the full bevel, charging the honing wheels with compound" />
        <figcaption>Wrong (tip only), correct (full bevel), and charging the wheels with compound</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p05_02.jpg" alt="Honing the gouge bevel rolling the jig, moving sideways across the leather, and honing the inside freehand" />
        <figcaption>Hone the bevel in the jig; hone the inside of the flute freehand</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h4>V-tools</h4>
      <p>A straight V-tool is two small chisels meeting at a keel. Grind each wing as a flat bevel in the SVS-38, rolling to the keel and treating the keel as a tiny gouge so it ends up slightly rounded on the outside rather than a hard corner (a sharp corner at the keel leaves a wedge of steel behind the edge that will not cut). Hone the outside in the jig and the inside with a slip or the profiled leather wheel. The full V-tool procedure in the handbook&rsquo;s SVD-186 chapter applies unchanged.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-htk-1006/svs38_p05_03.jpg" alt="A straight V-tool" />
        <figcaption>V-tools: grind and hone as described for the SVD-186 R gouge jig</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>Power-carver cutters</h3>
      <p>Gouges and V-tools for reciprocating carvers (Arbortech, Automach, Flexcut power handles) are short straight-shank tools and go in the SVS-38 like any carving gouge. Clamp on the shank, not the collet taper, and keep the angles the maker specifies; they are typically steeper than hand gouges because of the impact.</p>
      <div class="video">
        <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/gJTXMrHJQ88" title="Sharpen short edge tools with the Tormek Short Tool Jig SVS-32" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
        <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>Sharpen short edge tools with the Tormek Short Tool Jig SVS-32</em> (predecessor of the SVS-38; identical technique) (1:39)</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Honing Knives and Deburring</h2>
      <p>Grinding produces an edge with a burr on it; honing removes the burr and polishes the bevel so the apex is clean. On the T-8 that is the LA-220 leather honing wheel charged with Tormek honing compound (preparation and oiling are on the T-8 page). The rules are the same for every tool in this kit:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Edge trailing, always.</strong> The leather wheel turns away from the edge. Presenting an edge into the leather will cut the wheel and can throw the tool.</li>
        <li><strong>Same angle as the grind.</strong> Hone at a higher angle and you create a micro-bevel that is blunter than you intended; hone at a lower angle and you polish the shoulder and never reach the apex. A thin smear of marker on the bevel tells you where the leather is touching.</li>
        <li><strong>Light pressure.</strong> Leather compresses. Leaning on it wraps the leather around the apex and rounds it, which is the fastest way to undo a good grind.</li>
        <li><strong>Alternate sides, few strokes.</strong> The burr flips side to side as you hone; alternating weakens it until it drops off. Three or four light passes a side, then check, is plenty.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Knives</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Take the knife out of the jig. Stand so the wheels turn away from you with the honing wheel on your left (turn the machine around if needed).</li>
        <li>Lay the bevel of one side on the leather with the handle slightly raised so the whole bevel contacts, and draw the blade across the wheel from heel to tip, rotating the handle up as you reach the curve, exactly as when grinding but with the edge trailing.</li>
        <li>Repeat on the other side. Alternate until a fingertip stroked off the back of the blade across the edge feels no burr on either side.</li>
        <li>Finish with one or two feather-light strokes per side, then draw the edge through a block of end-grain softwood to strip any wire edge that is hanging on, and give it one last pass per side on the leather.</li>
        <li>Test on paper. A properly honed chef&rsquo;s knife will push-cut printer paper from heel to tip without slicing; if it catches at one spot, there is still burr or a flat there. Go back to the leather, not the stone.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Short knives in the SVM-45 or SVM-00 can be honed in the jig: move the universal support to the honing side in the horizontal sleeves, set it by the Marker Method so the leather touches the whole bevel, and hone each side with the stop pressed to the support. This is the way to keep a carving knife&rsquo;s flat bevel flat.</p>
      <div class="note">
        Very hard stainless steels (powder metallurgy knife steels) throw a stubborn burr that bends rather than breaks. For these, more alternating passes at very light pressure beat harder pressure every time, and the end-grain trick is essential.
      </div>
      <h3>Scissors</h3>
      <p>Not honed. Deburr on end grain and leave the fine-stone finish on the bevel; see the SVX-150 section.</p>
      <h3>Axes</h3>
      <p>Honed freehand on the leather wheel, away from the edge, both faces, rolling slightly to cover a convex bevel. A polished axe bevel is not vanity: it stops the bevel sticking in the cut and is what makes a carving axe leave a finished surface.</p>
      <h3>Chisels and gouges in the SVS-38</h3>
      <p>Honed with the tool still in the jig, support horizontal on the honing side, set with the Marker Method. Back of a chisel freehand and tangent to the wheel; inside of a gouge freehand on the profiled wheel or a slip. Detail is in the SVS-38 section.</p>

